    "content": "Palm Sunday, celebrated the Sunday before Easter (e.g., March 29, 2026), commemorates Jesus's humble entry into Jerusalem on a donkey, fulfilling prophecy. It marks the start of Holy Week with processions, waving palm branches, and services honoring Jesus as King. The donkey symbolizes peace and humility, while the palms represent victory. The Biblical StoryAccording to the Gospels, Jesus arrived in Jerusalem amid great anticipation following the raising of Lazarus, as described by Bible Study Tools. The Donkey: Jesus purposefully chose to ride a young donkey (or colt) rather than a warhorse. This fulfilled Zechariah's prophecy, signaling that He was a king coming in peace, not through force, say GotQuestions.org and YouTube. The Palms: People celebrated his arrival by spreading their cloaks and palm branches on the road, shouting \"Hosanna! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!\". The palm branches are recognized as a sign of victory and honor, according to Baylor Magazine and Trinity Church. Celebrations Today Worship and Processions: Churches often start with a procession where congregants hold blessed palm fronds, symbolizing their welcome of Christ. \"Palm Sunday of the Lord's Passion\": Services often combine the theme of joy at his arrival with the somber announcement of his imminent death, focusing on the Passion narrative, notes EWTN Norge and Catholic Online. Global Traditions: In some traditions, branches are taken home to be placed behind crucifixes or kept until the next Lent (when they are burned to make ashes). In other regions, church members may walk in public processions through streets, as seen in Wikipedia and The Seattle Times. When is Palm Sunday?Palm Sunday occurs on the Sunday immediately preceding Easter. Because Easter's date changes based on the lunar calendar, Palm Sunday typically falls between March 22 and April 25, according to Austin American-Statesman and Christianity.com.",
